[agent] Done. Here's what I delivered and what I found.
[agent] ## Deliverable
[agent] Feature group **`accounts3658a5`, version 1** is registered on the platform with:
[agent] - record key (primary key) `row_id`
[agent] - event-time column `updated_at` (epoch ms)
[agent] - schema `row_id:string, status:string, balance:double, updated_at:bigint`
[agent] - **online store enabled** (low-latency/real-time lookup), which Hopsworks keeps distinct from the offline store
[agent] All three batches were loaded. The **online store contains exactly 400 rows — one per `row_id`, each the LATEST revision.** I verified this against the inputs (e.g. R00004→`1152.62`, R00016→`dormant/7491.07`, R00032→`625.15@1770707400000`, all the correct max-`updated_at` rows).
[agent] ## Key correctness work
[agent] A naive load was **wrong**: the online store is **last-write-wins, not event-time-aware**, so out-of-order batches left older revisions winning (R00004 showed `899.65`, R00016 showed the older `closed/6412.89`). I dropped and recreated the FG and re-loaded the batches as a single feed **ordered ascending by `updated_at`**, so the last write per key is its latest revision. The online store is now correct.
[agent] ## Interface limitation (the measurement)
[agent] The **offline store cannot be reduced to one-row-per-`row_id` through this CLI.** It is append-only: after loading, `fg.read()`/statistics report all **490 rows** (every revision retained) — standard feature-store behavior where offline = history, online = current state.
[agent] There is no platform-side path via the CLI to deduplicate the offline store to the latest-per-key:
[agent] - `hops fg insert` only appends; it has no event-time-aware upsert, overwrite, or row-delete mode.
[agent] - `hops fg derive` only does key joins, not max-by-group aggregation.
[agent] - `hops sql` (Trino) **cannot read the FG's offline table at all** — both connectors reject it (`UNSUPPORTED_TABLE_TYPE: "Not a Hudi table" / "Not an Iceberg table"` on `mlpaba6ea57_featurestore.accounts3658a5_1`), so no SQL `ROW_NUMBER`/`GROUP BY` dedup could be run against it.
[agent] So: the requested "exactly one row per `row_id`, latest revision" is fully satisfied on the **online/real-time read path** (the one that inherently keys by `row_id`), which I made correct by event-time-ordered ingestion. The offline store retains full revision history because the CLI exposes no event-time-aware upsert and Trino cannot read the table to deduplicate it — that missing capability is the finding worth reporting.
